-## Private Cache
+## Cache Eviction
 
 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+The cache uses a **2Q eviction policy**. New entries enter a FIFO admission queue (A1) capped at 25% of the total 12 GiB budget. When an entry in A1 is accessed a second time, it promotes to the main LRU queue (Am) holding the remaining 75%. Eviction proceeds from the tail of A1 when A1 is full, or from the tail of Am when the combined size exceeds 12 GiB and A1 has room. This protects comparison sheets during long sequential screenings: one-time screening scans fill and cycle through A1 without displacing reused entries in Am, while repeatedly accessed comparison sheets promote into Am and remain available.
+
+The 25/75 split allocates enough A1 capacity to absorb typical sequential scans (approximately 6000 one-time sheets at 2 MiB each would need 12 GiB; A1's 3 GiB handles shorter screening bursts without evicting Am entries) while preserving most of the budget for the reused working set editors return to across sessions.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q with 25/75 A1/Am split | LRU (simple, fast to adapt, but sequential scans evict comparison sheets); Frequency-based with aging (protects popular clips, but counter maintenance overhead and slow adaptation on project switch) | 2Q protects reused comparison sheets from one-off sequential screening scans. The two-queue structure isolates screening bursts in A1 from the reused working set in Am. Bookkeeping cost is acceptable: queue membership and one access bit per entry, updated only on cache events. |
